PROPOSAL='''How did American ships receive protection before they possessed the papers needed to claim it? Comparing treaties with Algiers, Tripoli, and Tunis from 1795 to 1816, this paper distinguishes passport distribution, temporary credentials for purchased prizes, and convoy declarations. Richard O'Brien's account of the brig Mary in 1798 connects these arrangements to contested seizure, defective passports, and a proposed period of overlapping validity. The paper argues that provisional recognition made American protection depend on North African institutions and helped shape domestic documentary standards. A reproducible source register supports the comparison and public interpretation.'''
